---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Accessibility — JopChi"
  description="How JopChi makes a two-word puzzle playable without relying on colour: Geometry mode, validated palettes, and an honest account of what is not built yet."
>
  <article aria-labelledby="a11y-heading">
    <p class="section-label">Inclusive design</p>
    <h1 id="a11y-heading">Accessibility</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      JopChi hides two words at once. Almost every accessibility decision below
      falls out of that one fact — because the thing a player has to perceive is
      not <em>what colour is this tile</em>, it is <strong>which of two words
      does this letter belong to</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="page-intro">
      That question has a natural answer in space (left and right), in shape
      (which half of a diagonal), and in language (&ldquo;the left word&rdquo;).
      It has a poor answer in hue. So hue is the one channel we refuse to depend
      on.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="principle-heading">
      <h2 id="principle-heading">The principle</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <blockquote class="a11y-quote">
          <p>
            Accessibility gives equal access to the same information — never
            more, and never less.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          This cuts both ways, and the second direction is the one that is easy
          to get wrong. Before the Split, JopChi deliberately hides <em>which</em> word a
          misplaced letter belongs to. That ambiguity is
          the central mechanic.
        </p>
        <p>
          Internally we always know the answer, so it would be trivial to pan a
          misplaced letter&rsquo;s earcon to the left, or draw its glyph in the
          left half. <strong>We deliberately throw that information away.</strong> A
          blind player must not be told something a sighted player cannot see.
          Doing otherwise would be unfairness dressed as inclusion. A test
          asserts it stays that way.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="geometry-heading">
      <h2 id="geometry-heading">Geometry mode — reading the board without colour</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          Each tile is bisected corner to corner. <strong>Lower-left is Word A,
          upper-right is Word B.</strong> Three orthogonal channels carry the
          state, any one of which is sufficient on its own:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption class="sr-caption">Channels that encode tile state in Geometry mode</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Channel</th><th scope="col">What it encodes</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Position</th>
                <td>Which half of the diagonal is marked &rarr; which word</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Shape</th>
                <td>Diamond = locked in both &middot; ring = misplaced &middot; empty = absent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Stroke</th>
                <td><strong>Dashed = unresolved &middot; solid = resolved</strong></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          An ambiguous misplaced letter is drawn as a <em>literally incomplete
          outline</em> centred on the seam — belonging to neither half yet — and
          it closes and moves into its half when the Split resolves it. The
          Split becomes legible with no colour and no sound at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three visual modes are available in Settings: <strong>Classic</strong>, <strong>Geometry</strong>, and <strong>High Contrast Geometry</strong>,
          which thickens keylines and drops the softened halo for a harder edge.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="why-geometry-heading">
      <h2 id="why-geometry-heading">Why Geometry has to exist</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          We measured the shipped Classic palette to see how far apart Word A and
          Word B actually sit under different kinds of colour vision:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption class="sr-caption">Measured colour separation between Word A and Word B in the Classic palette</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Vision</th><th scope="col">Word A vs Word B</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Normal</th><td>&Delta;E 33</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Protanopia</th><td>&Delta;E 13</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Deuteranopia</th><td class="bad">&Delta;E 3</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Greyscale</th><td class="bad">&Delta;E 2</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Blue and purple become the same colour. <strong>No palette swap
          rescues a design that encodes two words as two hues</strong> — the
          shapes have to carry it. Classic stays pixel-identical for existing
          players, and a test asserts that this failure <em>persists</em>,
          because it is the justification for the whole feature.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="colorblind-heading">
      <h2 id="colorblind-heading">Colourblind-friendly palettes</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>Six palette options ship in Settings:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Classic</strong> — blue and purple</li>
          <li><strong>Green-weak</strong> — blue and amber; the most common deficiency</li>
          <li><strong>Red-weak</strong> — blue and amber, warmer</li>
          <li><strong>Blue-weak</strong> — red and cyan</li>
          <li><strong>No colour</strong> — four brightness steps, for total colour loss</li>
          <li><strong>Custom</strong> — choose each colour yourself, checked as you go</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Colour vision deficiency is not one condition, and a player often knows
          which hues they can separate better than any preset does. Each preset
          is validated against normal vision plus its own target deficiency —
          not against all of them at once, since red-green and blue-yellow
          deficiencies lose opposite axes, and satisfying every one forces the
          board onto a pure luminance ladder. That ladder already exists as
          the <strong>No colour</strong> preset, for the people who need it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="validator-heading">
      <h2 id="validator-heading">The colour picker checks itself</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          An unchecked picker is a <strong>hazard</strong>, not a feature —
          nothing stops someone choosing two colours that turn out identical to
          them mid-game. So every palette, preset or custom, goes through the
          same check, and the result is shown rather than hidden.
        </p>
        <p>
          The settings screen <strong>simulates the real board</strong> through
          protanopia, deuteranopia, tritanopia and greyscale, using the same tile
          glyphs the game renders, so the preview cannot drift from the game. The
          person choosing usually cannot see the failure mode themselves; being
          told a verdict is not the same as seeing it.
        </p>
        <h3>Why &Delta;E and not WCAG contrast</h3>
        <p>
          The first validator required a 3:1 WCAG contrast ratio between all four
          tile states. Every palette failed, including hand-picked ones, and the
          reason was arithmetic: four states each 3:1 apart needs 3&sup3; = 27:1
          across the range, and the maximum possible ratio is 21:1. <strong>The bar was unreachable by construction.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          WCAG contrast is also the wrong instrument here. It answers &ldquo;can
          I read text on this background&rdquo; — a pure luminance question. The
          question on a game board is &ldquo;are these two swatches telling me
          different things&rdquo;, where hue and chroma carry real information
          right up until a deficiency removes them. The validator uses <strong>CIE76
          &Delta;E in Lab</strong>, which counts luminance <em>and</em> hue and
          degrades correctly when a simulation collapses an axis. The threshold is 25, comfortably above &ldquo;noticeable&rdquo;
          (~2.3).
        </p>
        <h3>Swatches, not a colour wheel</h3>
        <p>
          A hue/saturation wheel needs precise dragging — poor with motor
          impairments, effectively unusable with a screen reader. Discrete
          swatches are a list of buttons, which VoiceOver handles natively. They
          are labelled by <strong>role and position</strong> (&ldquo;Left word,
          option 7 of 24&rdquo;), never by colour name, because
          &ldquo;blue&rdquo; is not useful information to the people most likely
          to be on this screen.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="motion-heading">
      <h2 id="motion-heading">Reduced motion</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          Tiles watch the iOS <strong>Reduce Motion</strong> setting and respond
          live, without a restart. When it is on, the tile reveal drops its 3D
          rotation entirely and becomes a plain cross-fade. Turning the setting
          on or off mid-game takes effect immediately.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sharing-heading">
      <h2 id="sharing-heading">Sharing without colour</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          A shared grid is the one artefact that lands in someone <em>else&rsquo;s</em> hands, and the sender cannot know how they see colour. JopChi&rsquo;s
          share format uses shapes rather than coloured squares, carrying
          identical information with no hue at all — and because it is keyed off
          tile state rather than tile colour, it preserves the
          resolved/unresolved distinction that colour alone cannot express. A
          test asserts no coloured emoji can appear in share output.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="verified-heading">
      <h2 id="verified-heading">How this is verified</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          Screenshots prove a thing once, for one board, on one device. These
          checks are structural and keep proving themselves as the game changes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Classic is untouched</strong> — every colour &times; every state renders identically with and without accessibility state, plus a guard that Geometry genuinely differs</li>
          <li><strong>All states distinguishable without colour</strong> — shape signatures are compared with every colour stripped; no two may collide</li>
          <li><strong>Ambiguity is never leaked</strong> — pre-Split misplaced letters must stay ambiguous</li>
          <li><strong>Every preset is usable</strong> — each validated against its own target deficiency</li>
          <li><strong>The picker is not a trap</strong> — a search of the real swatch list proves valid combinations are reachable, including one that survives total colour loss</li>
          <li><strong>Share carries no colour</strong></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="progress-heading">
      <h2 id="progress-heading">Still in progress</h2>
      <div class="card content-card in-progress">
        <p>
          Stated plainly, because a partial accessibility feature that claims
          completeness is worse than one that does not:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Stereo Rift audio.</strong> The state model, pan mapping and
            sweep semantics are implemented and tested; the sound engine is not.
            This needs pre-panned stereo assets and a native audio dependency.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Haptics.</strong> The vocabulary is designed, not yet wired.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>VoiceOver labels for the board.</strong> Buttons and controls
            throughout the app are labelled today, and the functions that
            describe a tile and summarise a row are written and tested — but they
            are not yet attached to the board itself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accessible Split placement.</strong> Needs an adjustable
            control and its announcements.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dynamic Type on the board.</strong> Tiles currently pin their
            font size so the grid cannot break its layout. Scaling the board with
            the system text size is not solved yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Switch Control and hardware keyboard.</strong> Not yet a
            deliberately designed path through the board.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="feedback-heading">
      <h2 id="feedback-heading">Feedback</h2>
      <div class="card content-card">
        <p>
          This site&rsquo;s own colour pairs are built to meet WCAG 2.2 AA, with
          a visible focus ring, a skip link, and reduced-motion support
          throughout.
        </p>
        <p>
          If something here does not work for you — on the site or in the app —
          we want to know. Email <a href="mailto:support@jopchi.com">support@jopchi.com</a>.
          We aim to respond within 2 business days.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  article { max-width: 52rem; }

  .page-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 1rem 0 1rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .page-intro:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  section h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .content-card {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .a11y-quote {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: 0.25rem 0 0.25rem 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .a11y-quote p {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  /* Wide tables must scroll in their own container, never the page */
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  th, td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.625rem 0.875rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  thead th {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  td.bad { color: #fca5a5; font-weight: 600; }

  /* Visually hidden, still announced */
  .sr-caption {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .in-progress {
    border-color: #fbbf24;
    background: rgba(251, 191, 36, 0.08);
  }

  .in-progress li { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .in-progress strong { color: #fde68a; }

  .content-card ul { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
</style>
